/* ---------- Biodiversity and Evolution ---------- */
KX("t11s1", [
["c", 2, "In the scientific name Homo sapiens, which word is the genus?", ["Homo", "sapiens", "Both words", "Neither word"], "The first word, with a capital letter.", "Binomial names give the genus first (capital letter) and the species second (lower case)."],
["d", 2, "A pond survey found 12 snails, 30 water fleas, 3 fish and 5 beetles. What percentage were arthropods (water fleas and beetles)?", ["70%", "35%", "60%", "10%"], "35 out of 50.", "Arthropods = 30 + 5 = 35 out of 50 animals, which is 70%."],
["c", 4, "Why do scientists use scientific names instead of common names?", ["Each species has one name used worldwide, so there is no confusion", "Scientific names are shorter", "Common names are always wrong", "Only scientists can say them"], "One species, many local names.", "Common names change between languages and places. One agreed scientific name avoids confusion."],
["d", 4, "DNA differences from humans: chimpanzee 1.2%, gorilla 1.6%, orangutan 3.1%. Which is our closest living relative?", ["Chimpanzee", "Gorilla", "Orangutan", "They are equally related"], "Fewest differences.", "The fewer the DNA differences, the more recently two species shared a common ancestor. Chimpanzees differ least."],
["i", 4, "When writing a dichotomous key, what should each step do?", ["Use one clear feature with two contrasting choices", "Ask about several features at once", "Use features that change with season", "Give three or more choices"], "Dichotomous = splitting in two.", "Each step should split the organisms into two groups using one clear, observable feature."],
["c", 5, "Whales and sharks both have streamlined bodies and fins. Why are whales classified as mammals?", ["Lungs, hair and milk", "They live in the sea, so they must be fish", "They are bigger than sharks, and size decides the group", "They have fins and a tail, like all fish"], "Look past features shaped by the same habitat.", "Fins and streamlining are adaptations to water, not signs of close relationship. Lungs, hair and milk show whales are mammals."],
["d", 5, "Base differences in one gene: species A–B 2, A–C 10, B–C 10, A–D 25. Which statement is best?", ["A and B are most closely related; A and D are least closely related", "A and D are most closely related", "C is the ancestor of all", "All are equally related"], "Smallest number = closest.", "Only 2 differences between A and B means a recent common ancestor. 25 differences between A and D means a distant one."],
["i", 5, "A key asks 'Is the leaf large or small?' What is the problem, and the fix?", ["'Large' is subjective: use a measurement", "There is no problem, because everyone agrees on what large means", "Ask about the colour instead, as it is easier to see", "Use more pictures instead of words, so no measuring is needed"], "Would two people always agree?", "Different users judge size differently. A measurable feature gives the same answer for everyone."]
]);
KX("t11s2", [
["c", 2, "Who proposed the theory of evolution by natural selection?", ["Charles Darwin (and Alfred Russel Wallace)", "Gregor Mendel, who bred pea plants in his monastery garden", "Louis Pasteur, who disproved spontaneous generation", "Robert Hooke, who first described cells under a microscope"], "He sailed on HMS Beagle.", "Darwin and Wallace proposed natural selection. Mendel discovered the patterns of inheritance."],
["d", 2, "Before industry, 2% of peppered moths near a city were dark. After industry, 95% were dark. By how many percentage points did the dark form increase?", ["93", "97", "47", "2"], "Subtract.", "95 − 2 = 93 percentage points. Dark moths were better camouflaged on sooty trees, so more survived."],
["c", 4, "How do bacteria become resistant to an antibiotic?", ["A random mutation makes some resistant; the antibiotic kills the others, so resistant ones survive and reproduce", "The antibiotic teaches the bacteria how to resist it", "The bacteria choose to change once they meet the antibiotic", "The patients become resistant, not the bacteria"], "The mutation comes first.", "Resistance comes from random mutation. The antibiotic selects for it by killing non-resistant bacteria, so the resistant ones multiply."],
["d", 4, "Rabbits exposed to a deadly virus: in year 1, 99% of infected rabbits died; in year 7, 25% died. What best explains this?", ["Rabbits with resistance survived and passed on their alleles, so resistance became common", "The rabbits learned to avoid the virus", "The virus stopped existing", "Rabbits made antibodies they inherited"], "Survivors breed.", "A few rabbits had alleles giving resistance. They survived and reproduced, so over generations a larger fraction were resistant."],
["i", 4, "Students model natural selection by scattering coloured paper 'moths' on newspaper and letting a 'predator' pick them up for 30 seconds. Which result is expected?", ["More of the newsprint-coloured moths survive", "More of the brightly coloured moths survive", "Equal numbers of each survive", "No moths survive"], "Camouflage.", "The predator finds the bright moths more easily, so camouflaged moths survive, modelling selection by predators."],
["c", 5, "A student says giraffes stretched their necks, so their offspring were born with longer necks. What is the best evaluation?", ["Wrong: changes during life are not inherited; giraffes already varied, and longer-necked ones survived and bred more", "Correct: stretching makes the neck longer, and this is passed on", "Wrong: giraffes' necks did not change at all over time", "Correct: stretching changes the genes in the neck cells"], "Acquired vs inherited.", "Stretching does not change the DNA in gametes. Evolution acts on existing genetic variation: longer-necked giraffes reached more food and passed on their alleles."],
["d", 5, "Percentage of resistant bacteria in a hospital: month 0 1%, month 6 10%, month 12 40%. The antibiotic was then stopped; at month 24 it was 25%. Why did it fall?", ["Without the antibiotic, resistance gave no edge", "The bacteria forgot their resistance once the antibiotic stopped", "All the resistant bacteria died at the same time", "The antibiotic is still being used in secret"], "Selection pressure removed.", "Resistance is favoured only when the antibiotic is present. Without it, resistant bacteria may grow slower and are out-competed."],
["i", 5, "In the paper-moth model, why should several different students act as the 'predator'?", ["To reduce one person's bias", "To make the experiment faster by sharing the work", "To use fewer moths in each round of the game", "So everyone in the class gets a turn at being a predator"], "One person might have a habit.", "Different predators give repeated trials. If the same pattern appears each time, the result is more reliable."]
]);
KX("t11s3", [
["c", 2, "What are fossils?", ["Preserved remains or traces of organisms from long ago", "Living organisms in rocks", "Rocks shaped like animals by chance", "Bones of animals alive today only"], "Ancient evidence.", "Fossils are the remains or traces (bones, shells, footprints, imprints) of organisms preserved in rock."],
["d", 2, "Rock layers from deepest to top contain: A fish, B amphibians, C reptiles, D mammals. Which fossils are the oldest?", ["The fish in layer A", "The mammals in layer D", "The reptiles in layer C", "All the same age"], "Lower layers formed first.", "In undisturbed rock, deeper layers are older, so the fish in A are oldest."],
["c", 4, "Why are homologous structures, like the five-fingered limbs of humans, bats and whales, evidence for evolution?", ["They have the same basic bone plan, suggesting a common ancestor", "They do the same job", "They are all used for swimming", "They evolved separately by chance"], "Same plan, different uses.", "The same set of bones adapted for different jobs shows these animals inherited the plan from a shared ancestor."],
["d", 4, "Differences in a protein (cytochrome c) from humans: chimpanzee 0, rhesus monkey 1, dog 11, yeast 44. Which conclusion is best?", ["Humans are most closely related to chimpanzees and least to yeast, supporting common descent", "Yeast is our closest relative", "Dogs are more closely related to us than monkeys", "Proteins tell us nothing about relationships"], "Fewer differences = closer relative.", "The number of differences rises with how distant the relationship is, matching what fossils and anatomy suggest."],
["i", 4, "How could scientists test whether two frog populations are separate species?", ["Test if they produce fertile offspring", "Compare their colours carefully under the same light", "Count how many frogs there are in each population", "Measure their size and compare the averages"], "The species definition.", "A species is a group that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring. If the two cannot, they are separate species."],
["c", 5, "Squirrels split by a deep canyon become two species. Which order of events is correct?", ["Isolation → no gene flow → different mutations and selection on each side → can no longer interbreed", "Can no longer interbreed → isolation → mutations", "Mutations → isolation → same species", "Selection → gene flow → isolation"], "Separate first, then change.", "Geographical isolation stops gene flow. Each population gains different mutations and adapts to its side, until they can no longer interbreed."],
["d", 5, "Finch beak depth averaged 9.2 mm before a drought. During the drought only large, hard seeds were left. Offspring of the survivors averaged 9.7 mm. What best explains this?", ["Deeper-beaked birds survived and bred", "Beaks grew deeper from cracking hard seeds, and this was passed on", "The drought caused mutations that made beaks deeper", "All finches grew bigger beaks because they ate more"], "Who survived?", "Deeper-beaked finches could eat the remaining seeds, survived and bred. Their offspring inherited deeper beaks: natural selection in action."],
["i", 5, "Why do scientists need beak measurements over several generations to show that finches have evolved?", ["To show the change is inherited and lasting, not just random variation in one year", "Because finches grow slowly", "To count more seeds", "Because one measurement is always wrong"], "Evolution = inherited change over generations.", "A change seen in one year could be chance or caused by diet. Showing it in offspring over generations shows it is genetic."]
]);

/* ---------- Life Processes in Plants ---------- */
KX("t12s1", [
["c", 2, "How do root hair cells take in water from the soil?", ["By osmosis", "By active transport", "By photosynthesis", "By phagocytosis"], "Water moves into the more concentrated cell sap.", "Cell sap is more concentrated than soil water, so water enters root hair cells by osmosis."],
["d", 2, "After 4 weeks, seedlings in a complete solution had a mass of 12 g; seedlings with no nitrate had 3 g. How many times heavier were the complete-solution seedlings?", ["4 times", "9 times", "3 times", "15 times"], "Divide 12 by 3.", "12 ÷ 3 = 4. Without nitrate, plants cannot make enough protein to grow."],
["c", 4, "How do root hairs increase water uptake?", ["They greatly increase the surface area for absorption", "They make water", "They pump water out", "They close the stomata"], "More surface, more uptake.", "Thousands of long, thin root hairs give a huge surface area for water and mineral uptake."],
["d", 4, "Nitrate uptake by roots: in aerated solution 50 units; in unaerated solution 10 units. Which conclusion is best?", ["Uptake depends on aerobic respiration, as it is mainly active transport", "Air blocks nitrate uptake", "Nitrate moves only by diffusion", "Roots photosynthesise"], "What does air supply to roots?", "Oxygen allows aerobic respiration, which makes ATP for active transport. With little oxygen, uptake falls sharply."],
["i", 4, "Why are seedlings grown in solution culture, not soil, when testing which minerals they need?", ["Solutions let you control the minerals", "Soil is too heavy for young seedlings to push through", "Seedlings grow faster in water, so results come sooner", "Soil has no water in it, so plants can't take up minerals"], "Control the variable.", "In solution culture, one mineral can be left out while all others are supplied, which is impossible to control in soil."],
["c", 5, "Plants in waterlogged soil often wilt, even though there is plenty of water. Why?", ["No oxygen for root respiration", "There is too much oxygen in the waterlogged soil, which poisons the roots", "The water blocks the stomata on the leaves, so they can't take in CO₂", "The leaves absorb too much water from the wet air and burst their cells"], "Roots need oxygen too.", "Water fills the air spaces in soil. Without oxygen, roots cannot respire aerobically, so active uptake fails and roots die, reducing water uptake."],
["d", 5, "After 4 weeks: complete solution, green leaves, 12 g; no nitrate, yellow, 3 g; no magnesium, yellow between veins, 8 g; no phosphate, purple tinge, 5 g. Which statement is fully supported by these results?", ["Lacking nitrate reduced growth more than lacking magnesium or phosphate", "Magnesium is not needed", "Phosphate is needed only for leaf colour", "Nitrate is part of chlorophyll"], "Use only what the data show.", "The no-nitrate plants had the lowest mass. The data do not show which molecules each mineral is part of, so the last option goes beyond the results."],
["i", 5, "In solution culture, the solutions are aerated and the containers are covered with black paper. Why?", ["Aeration gives roots oxygen for respiration; black paper stops algae growing in the light", "Both keep the solution warm", "Aeration adds minerals; paper adds darkness to the leaves", "To stop the plants growing"], "Roots need oxygen; algae need light.", "Air bubbles keep roots respiring. Covering the containers stops algae, which would use minerals and change the solutions."]
]);
KX("t12s2", [
["c", 2, "What is transpiration?", ["The loss of water vapour from leaves, mainly through stomata", "The uptake of water by roots", "The transport of sugar in phloem", "Making food in the leaves"], "Evaporation from leaves.", "Water evaporates from mesophyll cells and diffuses out of the stomata as vapour. This pulls water up the xylem."],
["d", 2, "In a potometer, the air bubble moved 24 mm in 4 minutes. What is the rate of water uptake?", ["6 mm per minute", "96 mm per minute", "20 mm per minute", "28 mm per minute"], "Distance ÷ time.", "24 ÷ 4 = 6 mm per minute."],
["c", 4, "Why does transpiration increase on a windy day?", ["Wind removes water vapour near the leaf, keeping the diffusion gradient steep", "Wind adds water to the leaf", "Wind closes the stomata", "Wind cools the leaf so it loses more water"], "What builds up near the leaf in still air?", "In still air, humid air collects round the leaf and slows diffusion. Wind blows it away, keeping the gradient steep."],
["d", 4, "Transpiration rates: 20 °C → 5, 25 °C → 8, 30 °C → 12 (dry air). At 30 °C: humidity 30% → 12, 60% → 7, 90% → 2. Which conclusion is best?", ["Transpiration rises with temperature and falls as humidity rises", "Transpiration falls with temperature", "Humidity has no effect", "Transpiration is fastest in humid air"], "Look at each variable separately.", "Higher temperature speeds evaporation and diffusion. Higher humidity reduces the water vapour gradient, so less water is lost."],
["i", 4, "Why is the leafy shoot cut under water when a potometer is set up?", ["To keep air out of the xylem", "To clean the shoot so the leaves transpire evenly", "To kill bacteria that would block the xylem", "To make the shoot heavier so it stays in the tube"], "Air bubbles break the water column.", "If air gets into the xylem, the continuous water column breaks and water cannot be drawn up properly."],
["c", 5, "A potometer measures water uptake, not exactly transpiration. Why?", ["Some water taken up is used in photosynthesis or kept in cells, so uptake is not quite the same as loss", "Potometers measure sugar moving in the phloem, not water in the xylem", "Plants do not lose water through the leaves, so uptake is all that happens", "All the water taken up is lost, so the two measurements are exactly the same"], "Not all water leaves.", "Most water taken up is lost by transpiration, but a little is used for photosynthesis and to keep cells turgid."],
["d", 5, "Over 24 hours, water loss from a tree peaked at 2 pm, but water uptake by its roots peaked at 4 pm. Why does uptake lag behind loss?", ["Loss from the leaves creates tension that pulls water up; uptake by the roots follows after this tension builds", "Roots take up water only in the dark, when transpiration has stopped", "The xylem is closed in the morning, so water can't move until the afternoon", "Water moves down from the leaves to the roots during the day"], "What drives the flow?", "Transpiration pulls water up the xylem. The trunk shrinks slightly as tension builds, and root uptake catches up later in the day."],
["i", 5, "Two plants have very different leaf areas. How can their transpiration rates be compared fairly?", ["Divide each rate by the plant's total leaf area", "Compare the rates directly", "Cut off leaves until they match", "Measure only the bigger plant"], "Rate per unit area.", "A larger plant loses more water just because it has more leaves. Rate per cm² of leaf allows a fair comparison."]
]);
KX("t12s3", [
["c", 2, "Which tissue carries sucrose from the leaves to other parts of a plant?", ["Phloem", "Xylem", "Epidermis", "Cuticle"], "Sugar goes in the...", "Phloem carries sucrose and amino acids (translocation). Xylem carries water and mineral ions."],
["d", 2, "Phloem sap collected by aphids contains 20% sucrose; xylem sap contains 0.01%. How many times more sucrose is in phloem sap?", ["2000 times", "20 times", "200 times", "0.01 times"], "Divide 20 by 0.01.", "20 ÷ 0.01 = 2000. Phloem carries sugars; xylem carries water and ions."],
["c", 4, "How are the stems and leaves of non-woody plants supported?", ["Turgid cells press against their cell walls, and lignified xylem gives strength", "By bones inside the stem, like the skeleton of an animal", "By air trapped in the stomata, which inflates the leaves", "By sugar in the phloem, which makes the stem stiff and heavy"], "Firm cells.", "Water makes cells turgid, which keeps soft tissues firm. Xylem walls with lignin also add strength."],
["d", 4, "Radioactive CO₂ was given to one leaf. After an hour, labelled sugar was found in the stem above and below the leaf, in the roots and in a fruit. Which conclusion is best?", ["Sugars move up and down in the phloem", "Sugar moves only downwards from the leaf to the roots, by gravity", "Roots and fruits make their own sugar, so the label came from elsewhere", "The xylem carries the sugar, along with water, from the leaf"], "Up AND down.", "Translocation carries sugars from sources (leaves) to sinks (roots, fruits, growing tips) in both directions."],
["i", 4, "In a ringing experiment, a ring of bark (including phloem) is removed from a branch. What is the best control?", ["A similar branch on the same tree with no ring removed", "A branch with all the leaves removed", "A branch from a different species", "A dead branch"], "Change one thing only.", "The control branch is the same except for the ring, so any difference is due to removing the phloem."],
["c", 5, "Removing a complete ring of bark eventually kills a tree, but not at once. Why?", ["Phloem is removed, so roots receive no sugar; they use stored food and die later, while xylem still carries water for a while", "The xylem is removed with the bark, so water stops flowing at once", "The leaves stop photosynthesising at once, because they lose their sugar", "The bark contains the chlorophyll, so the tree can't make food any more"], "Which tissue is in the bark?", "The phloem is in the bark, the xylem deeper. Roots slowly run out of stored sugar and die, then water uptake fails."],
["d", 5, "Sucrose concentration in phloem: leaf 20%, stem 15%, root 5%. What does this suggest?", ["Sucrose moves from a source (leaf) to a sink (root), down a concentration gradient", "Sucrose moves from roots to leaves", "Roots make the most sucrose", "Sucrose is the same everywhere"], "High to low.", "Sucrose is loaded into phloem in the leaves and removed in the roots, so concentration falls along the path from source to sink."],
["i", 5, "Why are aphids used to collect phloem sap?", ["Their stylet taps one sieve tube", "Aphids make phloem sap themselves inside their own bodies", "Aphids dissolve the xylem walls so the sap can flow out", "Aphids are easy to colour, so the sap can be traced through the plant"], "Nature's needle.", "Cutting the stem would mix contents of many cells. An aphid's stylet taps one sieve tube; cut off the aphid, and pure sap flows out of the stylet."]
]);

